\chapter{Binary formats, compression, hashing, and archives}
\label{ch:102}
\index{Binary formats, compression, hashing, and archives}

Binary-format and compression kernels work with explicit byte ranges and output limits. An archive adds an entry structure around bytes, which calls for separate bounds on names, entry counts, and decoded data. A checksum detects particular forms of accidental corruption. Cryptographic hash and authenticated encryption APIs have additional requirements and different security properties. Choose by the documented format or protocol requirement.

\section{Build a format oracle}
Start with a small valid fixture and record expected bytes, length and configured limits. Exercise a zero-length payload, a truncated header, a declared length larger than the available input, and an output buffer too small for the decoded result. Compare with an independent known-answer fixture when format interoperability matters. A round trip through a single encoder and decoder can preserve the same mistake. Verify ownership of returned storage before passing it to another kernel or a source bridge. Source text wrappers should not replace a binary representation when embedded zeros are valid.

\section{Derive a checksum that exposes a dropped zero byte}
For Adler-32 keep two running sums, initially one and zero. For each
byte, add it to the first sum, then add the new first sum to the
second; reduce modulo 65521. The high sixteen bits of the result hold
the second sum. For the three bytes 65, 0, 66, the successive pairs
are $(66,66)$, $(66,132)$ and $(132,264)$. Notice the middle step:
although the first sum did not change, the second advanced. The expected
checksum is therefore \OnxCodeTerm{0x01080084}. A text routine stopping
at the embedded zero would instead process just the first byte.

Divide the same array after its second byte. Updating from seed one
over the first chunk gives \OnxCodeTerm{0x00840042}. Feed that entire
value as the seed for the one-byte remainder. The final answer must
equal the three-byte answer, just as it must if the split comes after
the first byte. Starting again from one for each chunk would checksum
two independent messages, not the stream. As a separate interoperability
check, the manual gives the nine-byte Wikipedia vector and its expected
constant; a test need not derive its oracle from the implementation
under test.

What happens if a caller supplies a null address with a positive
length? This API returns the seed unchanged. That is also a possible
checksum result, not an out-of-band error. A container reader must
establish its input span before calling the checksum function; matching
the stored checksum cannot validate missing storage or archive offsets.

An empty but valid chunk, inserted between the two chunks above,
also leaves the accumulated checksum unchanged. A useful streaming
test includes that chunk expressly: the next nonempty update must
continue the previous sum, not reset it.

